<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Project-wide SpotBugs exclude filter. Applied to every module in
    addition to that module's own spotbugs_ignore.xml (if any). Wired
    via the parent pom's `<excludeFilterFile>` reference in
    pluginManagement and the `spotbugs-module-ignore` profile.

    Only categories that are structurally false-positive in this
    project belong here. Module-specific suppressions (a single
    class/field tuple) stay in the per-module spotbugs_ignore.xml.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>

    <Match>
        <!-- The project does not use Java Object Serialization anywhere.
             Persisted state is Jackson JSON end-to-end (config files,
             remote-RPC wire format, validation reports, etc.). Swing
             components and Jackson types inherit Serializable transitively
             but no class is ever written via ObjectOutputStream.

             Non-Serializable instance fields are marked `transient` to
             silence the javac [serial] lint. SpotBugs then flags those
             transient fields as "not restored on deserialisation" — true
             but irrelevant: the deserialisation path is never exercised. -->
        <Bug pattern="SE_TRANSIENT_FIELD_NOT_RESTORED" />
    </Match>

    <Match>
        <!-- Reported only since SpotBugs 4.10.0, which added *partial* JSpecify support:

               "Add partial support for org.jspecify.annotations.Nullable … aliased to the
                closest existing SpotBugs nullness annotations. This is not a complete
                implementation of the JSpecify spec."

             This project completed a project-wide JSpecify rollout, so 4.10 suddenly sees
             thousands of @Nullable annotations it previously ignored: the count went from 0
             under 4.9.8.3 to 313 under 4.10.4.0, across 17 modules. The flagged methods are
             overwhelmingly Lombok-generated @Nullable getters on the tolerant-wire-format
             CDISC rule model (CheckConditionLeaf.getValue, Rule.getCore, Operation.getName,
             RulePackage.getRules, … 100 distinct methods).

             NullAway already enforces exactly this contract with a COMPLETE JSpecify
             implementation, and more strictly than SpotBugs is being asked to: the NullAway
             profile sets -Xep:NullAway:ERROR with JSpecifyMode=true,
             AcknowledgeRestrictiveAnnotations=true and AnnotatedPackages=net.cumba, and the
             build is green under it. So these are duplicate coverage from an admittedly
             partial implementation, and the complete checker is the one to trust.

             THE OVERLAP IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT AN INVARIANT THIS FILTER ENFORCES. A SpotBugs
             Match cannot say "only when the callee is outside net.cumba", so this entry is
             unconditional and also silences the one category SpotBugs would genuinely add
             over NullAway: a null-returning callee OUTSIDE net.cumba, which NullAway treats
             as non-null because the package is unannotated.

             SO: RE-RUN THE CALLEE CLASSIFICATION ON EVERY SpotBugs BUMP. For each
             NP_NULL_ON_SOME_PATH_FROM_RETURN_VALUE in every target/spotbugsXml.xml, read the
             Method element whose role is METHOD_CALLED and confirm its classname starts with
             net.cumba. Any that does not is NOT covered by NullAway and must be triaged on
             its own merits before this filter is kept.

             The caveat and the protocol are ported up from cumba-oss-corej, which performed
             that classification (22 distinct callees, all inside net.cumba) and recorded the
             re-run instruction; the OSS twins carry their own measured counts.

             Revisit if SpotBugs completes its JSpecify implementation, or if NullAway is ever
             removed or demoted below ERROR. -->
        <Bug pattern="NP_NULL_ON_SOME_PATH_FROM_RETURN_VALUE" />
    </Match>

    <Match>
        <!-- Also new in 4.10.x, and 24 of the 24 occurrences are the same intentional shape:
             a package-private/protected member overridden as public in a nested formatter or
             helper class (every SASFormatFactory$*Formatter, for instance). Widening on
             override is deliberate here — the nested types implement a public SPI while their
             base declares the method protected. Nothing is "increased" that was meant to stay
             hidden. -->
        <Bug pattern="IAOM_DO_NOT_INCREASE_METHOD_ACCESSIBILITY" />
    </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
